        text: 'At a food plant, you coach the team leader of the packing hall. Near-misses, incidents that almost caused harm, are rarely reported: three this year, while the safety officer estimates dozens. Last month an operator reported one, and the team leader said in front of the shift: “How could you not see that?”',
        ask: 'What would you do next?',
        reveal: 'This is the anxiety zone: high standards, low safety, and the response killed reporting. Coach the team leader privately. Set the stage: “we need the near-misses to prevent the real accident.” Invite: at each start-of-shift meeting he asks “what nearly went wrong yesterday?” and shares one of his own. Respond: thank every report and fix one visibly.\n\nDraw the line clearly: skipping lockout, the procedure that locks a machine’s energy off before work, is blameworthy; reporting a near-miss never is. Track reports per week as the signal.',
